<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Certificate pinning for GoodTunes' own hosts (Tier-3 download hardening — see
  docs/cert-pinning.md and docs/roadmap.md).

  WHAT THIS PINS
  Only `goodtunes.music` and its subdomains (my.* / get.*). Every protected
  offline-download fetch that hits a GoodTunes host (object-storage masters at
  my.goodtunes.music/objects/...) is therefore tied to the GoodTunes TLS chain,
  so a rogue Wi-Fi / corporate proxy / mis-issued CA cert can't swap the bytes.
  Hosts NOT listed here (legacy Dropbox masters, Mux, Stripe) are deliberately
  left on normal system validation so this config can never brick them.

  WHY THE ISRG *ROOTS* AND NOT THE LEAF/INTERMEDIATE
  GoodTunes' certs are issued by Let's Encrypt, whose leaf + intermediate certs
  rotate every ~60 days. Pinning either would brick installs within weeks.
  Pinning the long-lived ISRG *roots* (valid into 2035/2040) survives every
  leaf/intermediate renewal. Three pins (one primary + two backups) are present
  so a single root or CA migration is never a single point of failure:
    - ISRG Root X2  (ECDSA root both subdomains currently chain to) — PRIMARY
    - ISRG Root X1  (RSA root, covers the RSA/R-series chain)        — BACKUP
    - ISRG Root YE  (new ECDSA root get.goodtunes.music already uses) — BACKUP

  EXPIRATION = ANTI-BRICK SAFETY NET
  After the `expiration` date the OS stops ENFORCING these pins and falls back
  to normal validation. That guarantees a stale, un-updated install can never be
  permanently locked out by a future CA change. Every native release should
  review and push this date forward. See docs/cert-pinning.md for the rotation
  runbook (add the new backup pin and ship an app update BEFORE switching CAs).

  NOTE: Android Network Security Config pinning applies to the platform HTTP
  stack (HttpsURLConnection / OkHttp), NOT to WebView content loads — which is
  exactly why the offline download is fetched natively via SecureKeyStorePlugin
  .pinnedDownload() instead of a WebView fetch(). It also does NOT affect the
  remote-origin WebView load of my.goodtunes.music, so the app shell can't be
  bricked by this file.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <domain-config>
        <domain includeSubdomains="true">goodtunes.music</domain>
        <pin-set expiration="2028-06-01">
            <pin digest="SHA-256">diGVwiVYbubAI3RW4hB9xU8e/CH2GnkuvVFZE8zmgzI=</pin>
            <pin digest="SHA-256">C5+lpZ7tcVwmwQIMcRtPbsQtWLABXhQzejna0wHFr8M=</pin>
            <pin digest="SHA-256">sCkq5UWXjg+7mKu9lMhhYF5bGLsy7VI/UNW3tccdR7w=</pin>
        </pin-set>
    </domain-config>
</network-security-config>
